  {
   "id": 431386298,
   "user": "tzachi-dar",
   "created_at": "2018-10-19T14:40:37Z",
   "body": "I guess that one can say that since xdrip was used against instructions this is not our fault.\r\nBut I believe that there are two things that we can do:\r\n\r\n1) not allow slope that is bigger than 30.\r\n2) Detect when a new sensor was started and also do that on xdrip.\r\n\r\nAny other thoughts suggestions?"
  },
  {
   "id": 431390631,
   "user": "MasterPlexus",
   "created_at": "2018-10-19T14:53:43Z",
   "body": "I would agree (clearly for the stop/start point as I see that often by others), for the slope I'm not sure. \r\nMaybe made that also customizeable, safe position standard activated, and make it only deactivateable in devmode for example."
  },
  {
   "id": 431453748,
   "user": "jamorham",
   "created_at": "2018-10-19T18:21:27Z",
   "body": "I think the maximum slope is already 1.5-1.6\r\n\r\nWouldn't exactly the same thing happen if the same thing was done with the official receiver? Eg swap the transmitter to a new sensor without informing it."
  },
  {
   "id": 431593300,
   "user": "tzachi-dar",
   "created_at": "2018-10-20T15:48:46Z",
   "body": "For the libre, it is not possible to switch a sensor without telling the receiver. The receiver says that a new sensor found and asks if to start it.\r\n\r\nLooking at my suggestion from above, I meant to say that we we should not allow *slope* bigger than 30.\r\nPlease note that in the case above, slope was 123, so no chance in the world, a hypo would have been found."
  },
  {
   "id": 431953336,
   "user": "jamorham",
   "created_at": "2018-10-22T19:39:55Z",
   "body": "Slope doesn't go above 1.5 in the items linked above which is in line with the constraints. Do you mean intercept?"
  },
  {
   "id": 432018416,
   "user": "tzachi-dar",
   "created_at": "2018-10-22T22:49:16Z",
   "body": "Sure intercept should not be bigger than 40 (or maybe even 30)."
  },
  {
   "id": 432231042,
   "user": "gregorybel",
   "created_at": "2018-10-23T12:44:30Z",
   "body": "detecting it is not an issue but then what we do then?"
  },
  {
   "id": 432314042,
   "user": "tzachi-dar",
   "created_at": "2018-10-23T16:12:55Z",
   "body": "In my opinion the first thing to do is in such case is simply print:\r\n\"Bad calibration data. Please calibrate again. You might have to wait 60 minutes from last calibration or replace the sensor\"\r\n\r\nSecond thing is for libre detect that a sensor has changed, and close the old one.\r\n\r\n"
  },
  {
   "id": 432451499,
   "user": "tzachi-dar",
   "created_at": "2018-10-23T22:59:17Z",
   "body": "I have created https://github.com/NightscoutFoundation/xDrip/pull/638 as a proof of concept of what I things that should be done.\r\n\r\n@jamorham, please approve, and I'll create the real code, and test it."
  },
  {
   "id": 433664437,
   "user": "tzachi-dar",
   "created_at": "2018-10-27T23:45:38Z",
   "body": "Here is another report that I got today. This one is even worth.\r\nIn this case, a 9 years old girl who calibrates twice a day got a slope of 300. \r\n\r\nThis has happened because of one reading, that was 300, while xdrip was showing bg=100.\r\nThis is probably a bad sensor (or transmitter). Nothing that we can do about it, but she has an animas ping that was showing an error, while we have been showing that all is ok, while we might cause her to keep injecting insulin.\r\n\r\n![image](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/7942372/47610335-0ffa4200-da53-11e8-853c-62730574dd02.png)\r\n![image](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/7942372/47610336-1b4d6d80-da53-11e8-9625-18380d724b6a.png)\r\n"
  },
  {
   "id": 433817161,
   "user": "gregorybel",
   "created_at": "2018-10-29T07:52:16Z",
   "body": "why are there so manycalibration data? \r\ndoes she never Restart a Sensor?\r\nthis looks like a bug in the calibration algorithm, this calibration should have been ignored.\r\nis she using an old version?\r\nI remember Jon adding noise check.\r\nor is maybe xdrip wrongly configured? there are a lot of options regarding calibration. "
  },
  {
   "id": 434127341,
   "user": "tzachi-dar",
   "created_at": "2018-10-30T00:17:56Z",
   "body": "Seems like they have been doing one/two calibrations a day, but not stopping/starting sensors.\r\nUsually it is not a problem since only last 4 days are used.\r\n\r\nI don't think that she is using an old version, I can verify it.\r\n\r\nSince they had raw of 100, while bg was 300, I don't think that ignoring this calibration was enough. We need to tell people in such a case to replace sensor.\r\nIf we still show 100, this is problematic.\r\nIf intercept is 200, this is still problematic.\r\n\r\nMy solution, is simple, and should tell people that they have a problem."
  },
